{"subjectId":"python","id":"generators-advanced","title":"Generators: send, yield from & Coroutines","group":"Advanced Python","order":40,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Generators are more than lazy lists: they can receive values, be told to stop, return a result and delegate to each other — the machinery that <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> grew out of.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Iterator class vs generator</h3>\n<p>Both objects below behave identically in a <code>for</code> loop. The generator version gets <code>__iter__</code>, <code>__next__</code> and <code>StopIteration</code> for free.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Countdown:                       # hand-written iterator\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.n = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.n &lt;= 0:\n            raise StopIteration\n        self.n -= 1\n        return self.n + 1\n\ndef countdown(start):                  # the same thing as a generator\n    while start &gt; 0:\n        yield start\n        start -= 1\n\nprint(list(Countdown(3)), list(countdown(3)))   # [3, 2, 1] [3, 2, 1]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-traffic-lights\"></i>Generator states</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from inspect import getgeneratorstate\n\ndef gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\ng = gen()\nprint(getgeneratorstate(g))      # GEN_CREATED\nnext(g)\nprint(getgeneratorstate(g))      # GEN_SUSPENDED\nlist(g)\nprint(getgeneratorstate(g))      # GEN_CLOSED</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GEN_CREATED</code></td><td>Created, body hasn't started — the first <code>next()</code> runs up to the first <code>yield</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>GEN_RUNNING</code></td><td>Currently executing (only visible from inside)</td></tr><tr><td><code>GEN_SUSPENDED</code></td><td>Paused at a <code>yield</code>, locals preserved</td></tr><tr><td><code>GEN_CLOSED</code></td><td>Finished, closed, or raised — further <code>next()</code> raises <code>StopIteration</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-send\"></i><code>send()</code>: values flow <em>into</em> the generator</h3>\n<p><code>x = yield value</code> hands <code>value</code> out and pauses; when resumed with <code>g.send(v)</code>, the <code>yield</code> expression evaluates to <code>v</code>. <code>next(g)</code> is the same as <code>g.send(None)</code>. The first call must be <code>next()</code> (or <code>send(None)</code>) to <em>prime</em> the generator.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def running_average():\n    total, count, avg = 0, 0, None\n    while True:\n        value = yield avg           # hand out avg, wait for the next value\n        total += value\n        count += 1\n        avg = total / count\n\navg = running_average()\nnext(avg)                           # prime: run to the first yield\nprint(avg.send(10))                 # 10.0\nprint(avg.send(20))                 # 15.0\nprint(avg.send(60))                 # 30.0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hand-stop\"></i><code>throw()</code>, <code>close()</code> and <code>GeneratorExit</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def worker():\n    try:\n        while True:\n            try:\n                job = yield\n                print(\"did\", job)\n            except ValueError as e:\n                print(\"recovered from\", e)      # the error is raised AT the paused yield\n    finally:\n        print(\"cleanup\")                         # runs on close() — GeneratorExit\n\nw = worker()\nnext(w)\nw.send(\"a\")                                      # did a\nw.throw(ValueError(\"bad job\"))                   # recovered from bad job\nw.close()                                        # cleanup</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>did a\nrecovered from bad job\ncleanup</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never swallow <code>GeneratorExit</code> and keep yielding — <code>close()</code> then raises <code>RuntimeError: generator ignored GeneratorExit</code>. Do cleanup in <code>finally</code> and let it propagate.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-forward\"></i>Return values &amp; <code>yield from</code></h3>\n<p>A generator's <code>return x</code> becomes <code>StopIteration(x).value</code>. <code>yield from sub</code> re-yields everything <code>sub</code> produces, forwards <code>send</code>/<code>throw</code> to it, and evaluates to its return value.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def read_block(lines):\n    count = 0\n    for line in lines:\n        if line == \"END\":\n            return count                 # becomes the value of \"yield from\"\n        yield line.upper()\n        count += 1\n\ndef document(lines):\n    it = iter(lines)\n    n1 = yield from read_block(it)\n    n2 = yield from read_block(it)\n    yield f\"blocks had {n1} and {n2} lines\"\n\nprint(list(document([\"a\", \"b\", \"END\", \"c\", \"END\"])))\n# ['A', 'B', 'C', 'blocks had 2 and 1 lines']</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>['A', 'B', 'C', 'blocks had 2 and 1 lines']</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def tree(node):                          # recursive delegation: walk a nested structure\n    name, children = node\n    yield name\n    for child in children:\n        yield from tree(child)\n\nt = (\"root\", [(\"a\", [(\"a1\", [])]), (\"b\", [])])\nprint(list(tree(t)))                     # ['root', 'a', 'a1', 'b']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-commit\"></i>Pipelines</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def parse(lines):\n    for line in lines:\n        name, amount = line.split(\",\")\n        yield name, float(amount)\n\ndef only_large(rows, limit):\n    return ((n, a) for n, a in rows if a &gt;= limit)\n\ndef total(rows):\n    return sum(a for _, a in rows)\n\nlog = [\"tea,40\", \"laptop,55000\", \"phone,18000\", \"pen,10\"]\nprint(total(only_large(parse(log), 1000)))    # 73000.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Each stage pulls one item at a time from the previous one, so the whole pipeline runs in constant memory — whether the input is 4 lines or a 40 GB log file.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>From generators to <code>async</code>/<code>await</code></h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">2001 — generators (PEP 255)</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>yield</code> makes pausable functions.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">2005 — <code>send</code>/<code>throw</code>/<code>close</code> (PEP 342)</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Generators become coroutines that can receive values.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">2012 — <code>yield from</code> (PEP 380)</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Delegation lets coroutines call sub-coroutines; asyncio was first built on it.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">2015 — <code>async def</code> / <code>await</code> (PEP 492)</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Native coroutine syntax, separate from generators, with the same suspend/resume idea.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">One-shot</div><p>A generator can be consumed <strong>once</strong>. Passing the same generator to two functions gives the second one nothing — materialise it with <code>list()</code> or use <code>itertools.tee</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does send() do, and why must you call next() first?</summary><div><p><code>send(v)</code> resumes a paused generator and makes the pending <code>yield</code> expression evaluate to <code>v</code>. A freshly created generator isn't paused at a <code>yield</code> yet, so the first resume must be <code>next(g)</code> (or <code>send(None)</code>) — sending a non-<code>None</code> value raises <code>TypeError</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you get a generator's return value?</summary><div><p>With <code>yield from</code> (the expression's value), or by catching <code>StopIteration</code> and reading <code>.value</code>. A plain <code>for</code> loop discards it.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Generator vs coroutine in modern Python?</summary><div><p>A generator (<code>def</code> + <code>yield</code>) produces a stream of values. A native coroutine (<code>async def</code>) is awaited and scheduled by an event loop; it can't be iterated with <code>for</code>. Both suspend and resume a frame, but they are different types with different protocols.</p></div></details></section>"}